WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.740
What determines Rabin–Karp's actual runtime?

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:04.980
We will keep rolling work

3
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.320
separate from candidate verification, count

4
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.220
the earlier collision example, and

5
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.460
distinguish useful expected behaviour from

6
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.620
a worst-case guarantee.

7
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.500
Use pattern twenty-five in text

8
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.400
one two two five, base

9
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.860
ten and modulus thirteen. There

10
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.560
are three legal windows, twelve,

11
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.080
twenty-two and twenty-five, whose hashes

12
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.320
are twelve, nine and twelve.

13
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.220
The first equal hash is

14
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.140
a spurious hit, meaning a

15
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.920
hash agreement whose strings differ.

16
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.060
Verification rejects after comparing only

17
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.780
the first digits, so that

18
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.800
candidate costs one character check

19
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.260
rather than the full pattern

20
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:39.600
length.

21
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.340
The middle window has a

22
00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.700
different hash, so it needs

23
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.600
no character verification. The final

24
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.240
window has an equal hash

25
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.220
and two matching digits, adding

26
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.600
two character comparisons and returning

27
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.260
the match.

28
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.200
The total is three window-hash

29
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.420
decisions and three actual character

30
00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.540
comparisons, with two rolling updates

31
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.140
after the first hash. A

32
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.440
hash comparison, a modular arithmetic

33
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.460
operation and a character comparison

34
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.280
are related costs, but they

35
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.860
are not interchangeable counters.

36
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.080
Let h be the number

37
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.400
of equal-hash candidates actually verified

38
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.900
during a scan. Each candidate

39
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.260
can require up to m

40
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.220
comparisons, in addition to the

41
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.200
linear-scale rolling work and the

42
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.180
pattern-length initialisation.

43
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.160
This yields big O of

44
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:26.720
n plus m plus h

45
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.460
m under constant-time word arithmetic.

46
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.800
The expression makes the assumption

47
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.700
visible: a small number of

48
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.380
candidate hits supports near-linear behaviour,

49
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.440
while many expensive candidates do

50
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:38.660
not.

51
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.900
Equal hashes do not all

52
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.900
have the same verification cost.

53
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.640
Some fail immediately, some fail

54
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.220
only after a long matching

55
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.160
prefix, and the first genuine

56
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.760
occurrence stops a first-match implementation.

57
00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:54.880
For worst-case behaviour, many windows

58
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.260
can pass the hash filter

59
00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.660
and disagree late in direct

60
00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.900
verification. Up to n minus

61
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.460
m plus one windows can

62
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.100
each require order m checks,

63
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.900
giving the familiar quadratic-style product

64
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:06.400
bound.

65
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.080
The pattern-length initial work remains

66
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.860
necessary even for a text

67
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.640
with a single complete window.

68
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.460
Keep it separate when stating

69
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.400
precise parameter-sensitive bounds instead of

70
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.360
silently assuming that n minus

71
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.760
m plus one is large.

72
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.980
A large prime can reduce

73
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.200
typical spurious hits, but cannot

74
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.280
eliminate the pigeonhole possibility of

75
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.640
collisions. Expected near-linear statements require

76
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.540
suitable assumptions about the inputs

77
00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.300
or hashing, not merely the

78
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.100
adjective large.

79
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.500
The deck also discusses measured

80
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.320
overheads such as function calls,

81
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.940
character conversion and modular division.

82
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.700
These observations explain why a

83
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.440
good asymptotic model need not

84
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.520
predict every small practical benchmark,

85
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.000
and they are not new

86
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.600
measurements of this implementation.

87
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.660
Things to take note of:

88
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:55.820
count rolling work and verification

89
00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.880
work separately, identify genuine versus

90
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.680
spurious hits, and state the

91
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.460
model behind expected bounds. Verification

92
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.640
keeps the algorithm exact, while

93
00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.100
the hash filter controls how

94
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.520
much of that verification work

95
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.100
it can avoid, do keep

96
00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:10.280
up.
